# Markdown Styling Conventions

This document describes how I like Markdown documents - including `.mdc` cursor rules - to be styled.

## Section Headings

Prefer Markdown headings for logical sections - don't just bold some text! A section is anything that introduces a logically different topic, or contains multiple instances of the same thing (a list, or a set of examples).

Do not **over-use** sections by adding "leaf" headings where a *list* or a simple *series of paragraphs* would do. Sections map to organization; leaf-level content that already lives inside a section probably doesn't need one.

**No clarifying parentheticals in headings.** A heading names its section. It is not a sentence and does not get a subordinate clause to soften, qualify, or explain it. If a heading seems to need a parenthetical, rewrite the heading to absorb it.

- **Wrong:** `## Configuration (Optional)`
- **Right:** `## Optional Configuration`

**Keep headings short and portable.** Headings get pulled out of their context constantly: into navigation sidebars, breadcrumbs, tables of contents, and the anchor text of links that point at the section. A heading that only makes sense sitting directly above its body is useless in every one of those places. Write headings that stand on their own.

- **Wrong:** `## A Few Things to Keep in Mind Before You Start`
- **Right:** `## Prerequisites`

### Examples

**Wrong** (underuses sections):

~~~markdown
## Quick Start

Prerequisites:
- git
- POSIX-compatible shell (bash, dash, zsh, etc.)
- Core Unix utilities (find, grep, cat, mktemp, etc.)

Installation:
```bash
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/ai-rizz.git
cd ai-rizz
make install
```

Personal rules only (git-ignored):
```bash
ai-rizz init https://github.com/example/rules.git --local
ai-rizz add rule my-personal-rule.mdc
```
~~~

**Correct** (fully leverages sections):

~~~markdown
## Quick Start

### Prerequisites
- git
- POSIX-compatible shell (bash, dash, zsh, etc.)
- Core Unix utilities (find, grep, cat, mktemp, etc.)

### Installation
```bash
git clone https://github.com/yourusername/ai-rizz.git
cd ai-rizz
make install
```

### Common Recipes

**Personal rules only (git-ignored):**
```bash
ai-rizz init https://github.com/example/rules.git --local
ai-rizz add rule my-personal-rule.mdc
```
~~~

## No Hard Wrapping

Never hard-wrap prose. Write each paragraph as one continuous line and let the reader's tooling wrap it to the window.

Hard wrapping - inserting newlines mid-paragraph to cap its visual width - buys nothing and costs maintenance:

- Markdown read by a machine (static-site generators, doc pipelines, anything that parses to HTML) collapses a single newline into a space. The wrap is invisible in the output.
- Markdown read by a human is shown in something that soft-wraps to the viewport: a browser, an editor pane, a rendered preview. The wrap is redundant.
- Either way the wrap reliably does one thing: it goes stale the instant you edit the paragraph, so every wording change drags a cluster of reflow noise into the diff.

The only place a newline inside a block is load-bearing is code, where whitespace can carry meaning - and code lives in code blocks, which preserve it regardless of this rule. So for prose there is no real exception: don't hard-wrap.

Line breaks that are part of a structure's syntax (table rows, list items, fenced code) are content, not wrapping. Leave them alone.

## Nesting Markdown Inside Markdown

Sometimes a Markdown document contains an example written in Markdown - documentation about documentation, a rule about rules, this very file. That puts a fence inside a fence, and the naive fix (count the backticks, add one more to the outer fence) is fiddly and easy to get wrong.

Use a tilde fence instead. Open and close the outer block with `~~~`, and inside it write ordinary triple-backtick fences exactly the way any author would - no escaping, no counting. A tilde fence only closes on tildes, so the backticks inside are left completely alone.

~~~markdown
## Install

```bash
make install
```
~~~

The block above is the entire technique: a `~~~`-fenced outer block whose body contains a normal ```` ```bash ```` fence, untouched.

You only count again if you need to show a tilde fence *itself* - a fence, inside a fence, inside a fence. That is rare. When it happens, bump the outer fence to four tildes (`~~~~`). Reach for this only when you actually hit it.

### Fallback: Indented Code Blocks

If the nesting gets so deep that even tilde fences are confusing, indent the block with a tab (or four spaces). Indented blocks use no fence characters at all, so they can never conflict:

	## No conflicts here
	```bash
	echo "works"
	```

The tradeoff is no language tag and no syntax highlighting. Use it only as a last resort - the tilde fence handles essentially everything.
